    "description": "Patent registration connects technical disclosure with territorial intellectual property rights. Understanding Iran’s patent activity requires separating applications, grants, international filing procedures, and evidence of commercial use.",
    "tags": ["Patents", "Intellectual property", "Invention", "Technology transfer", "Research"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:56:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:56:40Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Patent Registration in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian patent activity, the distinction between applications and grants, and the limits of international patent filings."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "rights-and-disclosure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a patent establishes",
        "content": "A patent is an exclusive right granted for an invention in exchange for disclosure of technical information. It concerns a defined technical solution, rather than recognition for scientific achievement in general. WIPO, the World Intellectual Property Organization, explains that common patentability requirements include novelty, an inventive step, industrial applicability, and sufficiently clear disclosure. The precise legal requirements depend on the jurisdiction. [s1]\n\nThese distinctions matter when reading accounts of Iranian research. A patent application records a request for protection; a grant records a different administrative outcome. Neither should automatically be described as a commercially successful technology. Patent rights can be licensed or transferred, but a claim that an invention has reached production needs evidence beyond the patent document itself. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "legal-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A changing legal framework",
        "content": "WIPO Lex identifies Iran’s Act on the Protection of Industrial Property 2024 as the latest version in its legislative record, with an adoption date of June 12, 2024. Its listed subjects include patents, utility models, industrial designs, trademarks, trade secrets, and enforcement. The record identifies the earlier 2008 registration act as repealed. [s2]\n\nThis is an important boundary for interpreting older English-language explanations: descriptions based exclusively on the earlier act cannot be assumed to explain the later framework. The legislative record establishes the change of law, but does not by itself verify current filing fees, portal procedures, examination practice, or the treatment of a particular application. Those operational questions remain outside this overview. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-statistics",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading Iran’s filing record",
        "content": "The series below counts applications attributed to applicants residing in Iran, combining resident and abroad filings. It therefore measures filing activity by origin, rather than applications received exclusively by Iran’s patent office. The distinction prevents foreign applicants filing in Iran from being confused with Iranian applicants filing overseas. [s3]\n\nThe available observations show a decline followed by a small rebound and another decline. They do not identify the causes, the commercial value of inventions, or the quality of examination. Applications and grants also belong to different administrative stages; comparing their totals in a single year would not establish the success rate of that year’s applicants. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "applications-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Patent applications attributed to applicants residing in Iran",
          "dataset_id": "iran-origin-applications",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["applications"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Patent applications, count",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Applications declined from 2020 through 2022, rose in 2023, and declined again in 2024. The final observation remained below the start of this five-year window."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "international-route",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International filing is not a worldwide grant",
        "content": "Patent rights are territorial. An Iranian patent does not automatically establish rights in other countries, and WIPO does not issue a universal world patent. [s1]\n\nThe Patent Cooperation Treaty, usually abbreviated PCT, provides an international application procedure. Its international stage includes filing, search, and publication; applicants subsequently pursue protection before the relevant national or regional offices. Those offices retain responsibility for granting patents. Consequently, an announcement of a PCT application should be described as a filing milestone, rather than an internationally granted patent. [s4]\n\nFor readers assessing an achievement, the useful questions are therefore specific: what document exists, which applicant and invention does it identify, which territory does it concern, and is it an application or a granted right? Evidence of licensing, manufacturing, or adoption answers a further question about what happened after protection was sought. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      }
    ],
